MKNOD(2)                       P32W Unix                        MKNOD(2)

NAME
       mknod - make a special file

SYNOPSIS
       #include <sys/stat.h>

       int mknod(const char *path, int mode, int dev);

DESCRIPTION
       mknod creates the file path with the given mode: the file
       type (S_IFCHR, a character device, or S_IFBLK, a block
       device) or-ed with the permission bits. dev is the device
       number, major * 256 + minor. Only the super-user may call
       it. The P32W devices are:

       type  major  minor  file
       c     1      0, 1   /dev/tty0, /dev/tty1
       c     2      0      /dev/null
       c     2      1      /dev/mem
       c     2      2      /dev/port
       c     3      0      /dev/rdisk, the whole card
       c     4      0      /dev/tty, the controlling terminal
       b     0      0      /dev/disk, the filesystem area
       b     1      0      /dev/ram, the RAM disk

       The permission bits are reduced by the file creation mask
       (umask(2)). The owner and group of the new file are the
       caller's effective IDs.

RETURN VALUE
       0 on success; -1 on an error, with errno set.

ERRORS
       EPERM   The caller is not the super-user.
       EINVAL  The type in mode is not S_IFCHR or S_IFBLK: a
               regular file or a directory (use creat(2) or
               mkdir(2)), or S_IFIFO (there are no named pipes).
       EEXIST  path exists.
       ENOENT  A directory in path does not exist.
       ENOTDIR A component of the path prefix is not a directory.
       EACCES  Search permission is denied on a directory in the
               path, or the parent directory is not writable.
       ENOSPC  No free inode, or no room in the directory.
       EFAULT  path is a bad address.
       ENAMETOOLONG
               path is 256 characters or more, or a component is
               longer than 28.

SEE ALSO
       mknod(8), open(2), stat(2), mkdir(2), mount(2)

BUGS
       A device number that names no device is accepted; opening
       the file then fails with ENXIO. There are no named pipes
       (FIFOs).
